### What is the effectively final rule?

Local variables used inside a lambda must be effectively final: either declared final or never reassigned after initialisation. This is because the lambda captures a copy of the value, not a reference to the variable binding.

### What does NOT compile?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class EffectivelyFinalError {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> amounts = List.of(
            new BigDecimal("1000"), new BigDecimal("2000"), new BigDecimal("3000")
        );

        // WRONG: count is reassigned inside lambda
        // int count = 0;
        // amounts.stream().filter(a -> {
        //     count++; // COMPILE ERROR! Not effectively final
        //     return a.compareTo(new BigDecimal("1500")) > 0;
        // });

        // WRONG: threshold is reassigned before lambda use
        // int threshold = 1000;
        // if (true) { threshold = 2000; } // reassigned!
        // amounts.stream().filter(a -> a.intValue() > threshold); // ERROR
    }
}
```

### What is the AtomicInteger workaround?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class AtomicCounterDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> amounts = List.of(
            new BigDecimal("1000"), new BigDecimal("2000"),
            new BigDecimal("3000"), new BigDecimal("500")
        );

        AtomicInteger checkedCount = new AtomicInteger(0); // reference is final
        BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("1500");

        List<BigDecimal> large = amounts.stream()
            .filter(a -> {
                checkedCount.incrementAndGet(); // OK: reference is final, value is mutable
                return a.compareTo(threshold) > 0;
            })
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Checked: " + checkedCount.get() + " amounts");
        System.out.println("Large: " + large);
    }
}
```

```text
Checked: 4 amounts
Large: [2000, 3000]
```

### Why is int[] unsafe in parallel streams?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ParallelSafetyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // UNSAFE: int[] in parallel
        final int[] unsafeCount = {0};
        IntStream.range(0, 100_000).parallel()
            .forEach(n -> unsafeCount[0]++); // race condition!
        System.out.println("Unsafe count: " + unsafeCount[0]); // often less than 100000

        // SAFE: AtomicInteger in parallel
        AtomicInteger safeCount = new AtomicInteger(0);
        IntStream.range(0, 100_000).parallel()
            .forEach(n -> safeCount.incrementAndGet()); // CAS: thread-safe
        System.out.println("Safe count: " + safeCount.get()); // always 100000
    }
}
```

```text
Unsafe count: 97834
Safe count: 100000
```

### What about instance fields?

Instance and static fields are not local variables and have no effectively final restriction. However, they share the same thread-safety concerns in parallel streams.

### What are the rules?

| Approach | Sequential safe? | Parallel safe? | Recommended? |
|----------|-----------------|----------------|--------------|
| int variable | Compile error | Compile error | No |
| AtomicInteger | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| final int[] | Yes | No (race) | Sequential only |
| Instance field | Yes | No (race) | Use with caution |

### What should a senior engineer know?

Effectively final is about the reference, not the value. `AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0)` is effectively final because the reference `counter` is never reassigned, but the value inside changes freely. The same principle applies to any final reference to a mutable object: final List, final Map, final StringBuilder. In parallel streams, always prefer atomic types over mutable containers.
